## Introduction
Designing multiscale porous structures in the range from nanoto micrometer represents a promising route to achieve optimal properties and performance because of their ability to interact with ions, atoms and molecules throughout the bulk of materials.[1-3] Multiscale porous structures offer unique properties to materials depending on the combination of the discrete pore sizes, e.g. micro- $(<2\ \mathsf{n m})$ and mesopores $(2-50\ \mathsf{n m})$ impart high surface areas and pore volumes, and provide size and shape selectivity, while macropores $(>50\ \mathsf{n m})$ impart facile mass transportation to active sites.[4]  Moreover, uniformity of pore size is an important factor that provides the desired functionality in materials for specific applications.[5-6]  In spite of these positive points, however, the development of strategies to control the trimodal (macro/meso-/micro-) porous structures with targeted pore size currently remains as a challenging goal.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are assembled from metal ions (or clusters) and organic linkers, possesses crystalline or amorphous frameworks with permanent open porosity.[7-8] Especially in crystalline MOFs, their long-range ordered networks offer uniform pore sizes and well-defined microporous structures.[7]  Constructions of hierarchical structures in crystalline MOFs is considered as an important strategy to realize controlled trimodal porous structures in materials.  In order to build macro/mesoporous MOFs, many efforts have been devoted.[9-18]  The major synthetic strategies include bottom-up sol-gel process[9-11], template-free [11-14] and templating synthesis,[15-16] replication process[17], and mechanochemical reaction methods.[18]  These approaches, however, are difficult to facilitate hierarchical crystalline MOFs with independently controlled macro/mesoporous structures.
Herein, we demonstrate our idea to elaborate monolithic materials with controlled trimodal porous structures.  The key design element in our strategy is formation of macroporous structures via the sol-gel process accompanied by phase separation, which is triggered by the short-range ordered selfassembly of MOFs.  The sol-gel process accompanied by the selfassembly-induced phase separation is derived from the idea of synthesis of macroporous silica monoliths.[19-20]  Since Nakanishi and co-workers reported macroporous silica monoliths via the solgel process accompanied by phase separation in 1991, various kinds of metal oxide, organic-inorganic hybrid, and organic polymer monoliths have been reported.[4,21]  Most of these materials were synthesized via the sol-gel process based on polymerization by chemical reactions.  In this study, we tried to apply this process to the self-assembly process of MOFs.  The key element in this process is that the self-assembly of metal ions (or clusters) and organic linkers form a transparent monolithic gel, which has a non-macroporous structure, in the absence of phase separation inducer.  However, in general, the high crystallization tendency of MOFs impedes such uniform gelation.  Opaque gels composed of aggregated crystallites coarse enough to scatter the visible light thus have been obtained in previous works.[10,22-23] Macroporous structure is not controllable in such cases, since the formation of macropores relies on the uncontrolled aggregation of coarse crystallites.  The controllable macroporous structure can be developed by spinodal decomposition, which is induced in fine enough networks composed of small crystallites consisting of the short-range ordered framework structure.  Thus, the formation of uniform, transparent monolithic gels is required in the absence of phase separation inducer, and well-defined, controllable macroporous structure can be developed by spinodal decomposition in the presence of phase separation inducer in such uniform gelation systems.  Subsequently, the original macropore skeletons with low crystallinity can be reorganized to long-range ordered high crystalline skeletons without disturbing the preformed macroporous structures. Furthermore, by choosing appropriate reorganization conditions, the mesoporous structures within the pre-formed macropore skeletons are expected to be controlled with different pore size distribution.
In this study, as a proof of concept, we focused on $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ -based UiO66 type MOF, which undergoes sol-gel transition by self-assembly of Zr clusters and terephthalic acid.[14,22-23]  The MOF UiO-66, with the formula $[{\cal Z}r_{6}{\cal O}_{4}({\sf O H})_{4}({\sf b d c})_{6}]$ ( $\mathbf{\partial}\ b d c=1$ ,4-benzenedicarboxylate), is one of the most important MOFs due to its high thermal, chemical, and mechanical stabilities,[24-25] and has been increasingly studied for a variety of applications e.g. catalyst,[26-27] catalyst support,[28-29] adsorbent,[30] gas storage medium,[14] and liquid or gas separation medium.[31-32] We used 2- aminoterephthalic acid $(B D C-N H_{2})$ as organic linker to obtain UiO$66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ because of its good solubility in solvent such as N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF).
## Results and Discussion
Figure 1 shows a schematic illustration of the synthesis of macrorous Zr-BDC- $N H_{2}$ metal-organic gels, which consist of the short-range ordered ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-66-N H}}_{2}$ .  Typically, $Z r{\mathsf{O C l}}_{2}{\cdot}8{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and BDC-NH2 were employed as inorganic precursor and organic linker, respectively.  Poly(propylene glycol) (PPG, $M_{\mathfrak{n}}=1$ 000) was used as phase separation inducer in DMF with the idea that the intrinsically hydrophilic Zr-BDC- $\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{\Lambda}^{[33]}$ has low compatibility with hydrophobic polymer.  The mixture of $Z r\mathsf{O C l}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , BDC$N H_{2}$ , and DMF was homogeneous and transparent at room temperature.  By adding PPG into the solutions, the yellow emulsions were obtained at room temperature.  After stirring in water bath at $60\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 min, the emulsions became homogeneous and transparent (Figure 1).  The solutions were transferred into the water bath at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , and as a result, so-called mismatched growth[10] of the Zr-BDC- $\cdot N H_{2}$ clusters into low crystalline networks only with short-range orders may have occurred, yielding macroporous opaque yellow gels (Figure 1). After washing with DMF, 2-propanol, and $n$ -hexane, the wet gels were dried at $40~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , and then $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a vacuum condition.
The morphology of interconnected macroporous structures of macrorous Zr-BDC- $\cdot N H_{2}$ metal-organic gels could be controlled by changing the starting compositions.  The PPG amount was used as a variable.  Without PPG, the transparent yellow gels were obtained, and the non-macroporous structure was observed (Figure S1A).  The formation and coarsening of the interconnected macroporous structures were observed with an increase of PPG amount (Figure 2A-C).  Figure 2D-E show the results of mercury intrusion measurements on the samples prepared with different amounts of PPG, $W_{\mathsf{P P G}}=550$ mg and 570 mg.  In particular, at $W_{\mathsf{P P G}}~=~580~\mathsf{m g}$ , the secondary phase separation[34] was observed in the skeletons (Figure 2C), and two kinds of macropores were obtained.  Thus, in the present range of starting compositions, it was possible to control the macropore diameter from $0.93~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ to $1.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Figure 2D-E, Table S2).  The influence of the amount of solvent DMF on the macroporous structure and the diffraction peaks in X-day diffraction (XRD) are shown in Figure S2 and S3, respectively.  Low concentrations of $Z r{\mathsf{O C l}}_{2}{\cdot}8{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and $B D C-N H_{2}$ suppress the phase separation tendency, and the appropriate amount of solvent was required to obtain well-defined macroporous structures in this system.
The diffraction peaks of the $Z r{\mathrm{-BDC-NH}}_{2}$ metal-organic gels $(w_{\mathsf{P P G}}=0\mathrm{~mg}$ , 550 mg, $570~\mathrm{mg}$ , and $580~\mathrm{mg},$ in XRD patterns show only negligible differences (Figure 2F, Figure S1B). Comparison of the diffraction pattern of the Zr-BDC-NH2 metalorganic gels and simulated pattern of UiO-66 indicates that the obtained (non-)macroporous samples consist of the short-range ordered UiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ .
The macroscopic appearance and the mesoscopic structures observed by a scanning electron microscope (SEM) of the macrorous Zr-BDC- $\cdot N H_{2}$ metal-organic gels $\langle W_{\mathsf{P P G}}=570~\mathsf{m g}.$ ) are shown in Figure 2G and 2H, respectively.  The macropore skeletons of the obtained sample consist of primary particles, and the sample had monolithic forms in centimeter-size.  Nitrogen adsorption-desorption measurements were performed on the samples prepared with the different amounts of PPG (Figure S1CD, Figure S4).  In the mesoscopic region, each macroporous Zr$B D C-N H_{2}$ metal-organic gel had a similar mesopore size distribution centered around $5\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S4 and Table S2).  On the other hand, the non-macroporous samples had apparently broader distributions around $8\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S1C-D, Table S1).  The mesopore size distributions are depending on the size of the primary particles, and the larger size of the primary particles should result in the larger mesopore size.  In addition, it has been reported that the crystallization and grain growth behavior of UiO66 are critically influenced by the solvent composition.[11,22]  In this case, therefore, it is considered that the grain growth is more significantly impeded in the solvent containing PPG than in the solvent without PPG, and as a result, the difference in the mesopore size distribution was observed.
The nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms of the macroporous Zr-BDC-NH2 metal-organic gels were identified as a combination of type I and type IV, which indicates the co-presence of micropores and mesopores (Figure S4).  The BET surface area of (non-)macrorous Zr-BDC- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ metal-organic gels $(w_{\mathsf{P P G}}=0\mathsf{m g}$ , $550~\mathrm{mg}$ , 570 mg, and $580~\mathrm{mg}$ ) are calculated to be $712{\sim}749~\mathsf{m}^{2}$ ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ (Table S1-2).  The high BET surface area also suggests the presence of microporous frameworks, which consists of the shortrange ordered UiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ .
The Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectra of the $Z r\mathrm{-BDC-NH_{2}}$ metal-organic gels prepared with $570~\mathrm{mg}$ and without PPG are shown in Figure S5.  The absorption bands of the two samples were almost the same in the range from 500 to $4000\ c m^{-1}$ .  In addition, the thermogravimetry-differential thermal analysis (TG-DTA) measurement shows apparent differences in DTA curves between the as-dried macrorous Zr-BDC-NH2 metalorganic gels $\langle W_{\mathsf{P P G}}=570\mathsf{m g}.$ and PPG (Figure 2I).  These results suggest that PPG can be almost completely removed by solvent exchange and both of the samples had the similar chemical structure (Figure S5), although the obtained samples prepared with $570~\mathrm{mg}$ and without PPG had different macro-/mesoporous structures (Figure. 2B and Figure S1A, C-D and Figure S4).  The energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) results show uniform distributions of Zr and $B D C-N H_{2}$ , and impurities were not observed (Figure S6-7 and Table S3).
The phase separation tendency is related to the miscibility of a binary polymeric system, which can be estimated using the FloryHuggins formula[35-37]  The Gibbs free energy change of mixing, $\Delta G$ is expressed as;
$$
\Delta G\propto R T[\{(\varphi_{1}/P_{1})|\mathfrak{n}\varphi_{1}+(\varphi_{2}/P_{2})|\mathfrak{n}\varphi_{2}\}+\{\chi_{12}\varphi_{1}\varphi_{2}\}]
$$
Here, $\varphi_{\mathrm{i}}$ and $P_{\mathrm{i}}\left(i=1,2\right)$ denote the volume fraction and degree of polymerization of component $i,$ respectively, and $\chi_{12}$ shows the interaction parameter, and $R$ and $\tau$ are the gas constant and temperature, respectively.  The former two terms in parenthesis represent the entropic contribution, and the last term the enthalpic contribution.  Results of TG-DTA measurement indicate that PPG can be removed by solvent exchange easily, thus PPG does not interact with $Z r{\mathrm{-BDC-NH}}_{2}$ strongly (Figure 2I).  This result is consistent with the low compatibility of the Zr-BDC- $\cdot N H_{2}$ and PPG. On the other hand, the mercury intrusion measurements show that the volume fraction of the skeleton remains almost the same with varied amount of PPG (Figure 2D).  In the case of the macroporous AlOOH monoliths prepared via the phase separation process,[38] the organic polymer, which was employed as the phase separation inducer, and AlOOH, are distributed as the major components to fluid- and gel-rich phases, respectively. The volume fraction of the macropores increased as the amount of the organic polymer increased.  In the present case, it therefore can be deduced that the phase separation inducer, PPG, was distributed both of the solvent- and gel-rich phases, and acted as a co-solvent.
Figure 3 shows the schematic representation of reorganization of the framework structures and control of the mesoporous structures in the skeletons.  There are some reports about acceleration of crystallization in UiO-66 type MOFs with acetic acid as a mediator.[39-40]  In this study, we used  the mixture of acetic acid and N,N-dimethylformamide (Vacetic acid / $V_{\mathsf{D M F}}=25/75)$
as the mediator solution.  As-dried and wet macroporous Zr-BDC$N H_{2}$ metal-organic gels were used in the solvothermal process for reorganization.  The mesoporous structure in the skeletons was coarsened as the grains grew due to the crystallization from low crystalline $Z r{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{B D C-N H}_{2}$ to ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ (Figure 3).  When the volume percentage of acetic acid was above $30\%$ , the macroporous structures were collapsed due to the strong tendency of dissolution of the monolithic gel in the solution.
Macroporous Zr-BDC-NH2 metal-organic gels were synthesized at four times larger scale $V_{\mathrm{DMF}}=28.8~\mathrm{mL}$ , $w_{\tt P P G}=2.26~\mathfrak{g}$ ) and the gels fabricated from the same solution were subjected to the solvothermal process in different conditions to investigate the structural changes before and after the reorganization process. The as-dried macroporous Zr-BDC-NH2 gels synthesized in the large scale (denoted as Zr-BDC-NH2 gel (Large-scale)) had welldefined macroporous structures and small primary particles (Figure S8A,D).  The diffraction peaks of the Zr-BDC-NH2 gel (Large-scale) in XRD patterns show only negligible differences with the samples synthesized in the small scale (Figure 2F and Figure 4A).  Although the BJH pore size distribution of the ZrBDC- $\cdot N H_{2}$ gel (Large-scale) obtained from nitrogen adsorptiondesorption measurements shows the mesopore distribution around $5\mathsf{n m}$ , which is similar to that of the small-scale samples (Figure S4B and Figure 4G), and the BET surface area and mesopore volume of the large-scale sample are smaller than those of the small-scale samples (Table S2 and Figure 4I).  It can be considered that the different kinetics of the solvent-exchange and the drying processes in the samples influenced the BET surface area.  This result indicates the microporous structures in the low crystalline Zr-BDC-NH2 gels are not stable enough during the solvent-exchange and drying processes.
The XRD patterns of the samples solvothermally treated in different conditions match well with that of simulated UiO-66, and the patterns of the samples solvothermally treated in different conditions are similar (Figure 4A).  Figure 4B-C show molecular models of UiO-66.  The XRD results confirm that the framework structures of $Z r{\mathrm{-BDC-NH}}_{2}$ gel (Large-scale) were reorganized from short-range ordered low crystalline structure to long-range ordered microporous structure (Figure 4A-C).  Figure 4D-E and Figure S8B-C show the SEM images of the solvothermally treated samples.  The UiO-66-NH2 gel had well-connected co-continuous macroporous structure, and structural gradient or inhomogeneity of the macroporosity could not be observed (Figure 4D).  The macroporous structures are almost unchanged during the solvothermal process in different conditions (Figure S8A-C and Figure 4E).
The skeletons of the solvothermally treated samples consist of the primary particles (Figure 4F and Figure S8E-F).  The transmission electron microscope (TEM) images of these samples revealed the trend of the primary particles growth (Figure S9).  The size of the primary particles observed by TEM is in the following order: UiO-66-NH2 gel (solvothermally treated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the wet gel) $>$ UiO-66-NH2 gel (solvothermally treated at
$140~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the dried gel) $>$ UiO-66-NH2 gel (solvothermally treated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the dried gel) $>>Z r$ -BDC-NH2 gel (Largescale).  Nitrogen adsorption-desorption results show that mesopore size distribution shifted to a larger pore size range with the increase of the size of the primary particles (Figure 4G-H). The modal sizes of the mesopore size distribution were shifted from 9 nm to 21 nm (Figure 4I).  The BET surface area of the crystalline macroporous UiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ gels are $859\sim915~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , which are higher than that of the $Z r{\mathrm{-BDC-NH}}_{2}$ gel (Large-scale) $(579\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , Figure 4I).  In addition, the mesopore volume is in the following order: $J i O\mathbf{-}66\mathbf{-}N H_{2}$ gel (solvothermally treated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the wet gel) $>\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66\mathsf{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ gel (solvothermally treated at $140~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the dried gel) $>$ UiO-66-NH2 gel (solvothermally
