treated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the dried gel).  The EDS results of the samples show the uniform distribution of $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ and $B D C-N H_{2}$ and similar chemical compositions (Figure S10-15 and Table S4-6).
Before the drying step, the diameter of wet $Z r{\mathrm{-BDC-NH}}_{2}$ gel (Large-scale) was $6.3~\mathsf{m m}$ , while that of as-dried sample was 5.7 mm.  The shrinkage of the sample during the drying process can be explained by the densification of the skeletons and the grains by the capillary force of the solvent.[41]  The densification of the grains and skeletons should limit the space for the particle growth, thus the solvothermal treatment of the wet gel at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ allowed more extensive growth than the dried gel at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure 4G-H, Figure S9).  In addition, the increase in temperature of the solvothermal treatment accelerated the grain growth, thus the solvothermal treatment of the dried gel at $140^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ allowed more extensive growth than the dried gel at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure 4G-H, Figure S9).  As a result, by choosing the starting material (wet or dried) and the temperature of solvothermal treatment, the modal sizes of mesopore size distribution were controlled from 9 nm to 21 nm in the present conditions (Figure 4I).
Uniaxial compression tests showed differences in mechanical properties of these as-dried samples (Figure S16, Table S7).  The stress-strain curves of the samples can be divided into three regions; (1) initial setting region, (2) linear elastic region, (3) yielding region.  The initial setting region $(\mathfrak{E}\colon0\sim1.6\ \%)$ , non-liner regions, is originated from the fact that the top and bottom surfaces of the samples are not perfectly smooth.  When further compressed, the stress-strain curve enters the linear elastic region, in which the Young's modulus of the samples can be calculated.  The Young's modulus of the samples is in the following order: UiO-66-NH2 gel (solvothermally treated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the dried gel, 100 MPa) $>$ Zr-BDC-NH2 gel (Large-scale, 75 $\mathsf{M P a})>\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}\mathsf{g e}|$ (solvothermally treated at $140^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the dried gel, 69 MPa) $>$ UiO-66-NH2 gel (solvothermally treated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ from the wet gel, 29 MPa) (Table S7).  The low crystalline Zr-BDC- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ gel had high Young’s modulus about $75~\mathsf{M P a}$ , and the solvothermally treated UiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ samples also had enough high mechanical stability comparing to the previously reported macro-/mesoporous MOF- or MOP-based gels.[42-43]  For the similar crystallinity and macroporous structures, the Young's modulus of the UiO-66-NH2 gels decreases as the mesopore volume of the samples increases (Figure4I, Table S7).  This tendency is consistent with the fact that increasing porosity, which accompanies decreased total area of the connections of the particles, reduces the mechanical strength of monolithic materials.[44]  When further compressed beyond the linear elastic regions, the stress-strain curve enters the non-liner yielding region, in which the curves can be classified to convex curves (Figure S16A-C) and downward convex curves (Figure S16D). This difference should be derived from the difference in the particle sizes and the contact area between individual particles. With the convex curves, the low crystalline $Z r\mathrm{-BDC-NH_{2}}$ gel showed the highest durability against strain in the yielding region before the gel was crushed, and the ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ gels (solvothemally treated from the dried gels) showed less durability against strain in the yielding region (Figure S16A-C).  This result implies that the crystallization of the UiO-66-NH2 network increased the particle sizes (Figure S9) and the packing of the particles in the skeleton during the compression became difficult. On the other hand, the UiO-66-NH2 gel (solvothermally treated from the wet gel) showed downward convex curve. This difference can be derived from an increase in the strength of particle connections and an increase in contact area between the individual particles in this sample.  The solvothermal treatment of the wet gel more significantly increased the particles size (Figure S9) and the contact area between the individual particles as compared to that from the dried gels, resulted in the difficulty in the packing of the particles in the skeleton.
## Conclusion
These results demonstrate a new strategy for designing trimodal porous structures in network-forming monolithic materials.  Selfassembly-induced phase separation process has been successfully applied to the sol-gel process of Zr-BDC-NH2 type metal-organic frameworks. Macroporous structures of the samples were controlled by freezing the transient structures of phase separation at different timing.  By choosing appropriate reorganization conditions, the low crystalline Zr-BDC-NH2 frameworks were converted to crystalline UiO-66-NH2 with controllable mesopore size distributions.  Extending this approach to other type of MOFs should permit the design of microporous structures with suitable meso-/macroporous structures for applications.  We believe that this two-step structural ordering process of MOFs can be extended to other type of MOFs, such as other zirconium-based MOFs,[23] aluminum-based MOFs,[10] iron-based MOFs,[9-10] and chromium-based MOFs,[45] because sol-gel transition behaviors of these MOFs have been reported. In addition, the acceleration and suppression of the crystallization tendency of the MOFs have been investigated over the past decade.[46-47]
